  "notes": "The research opens on the word \"Split\" — minor soul loops on album cuts, major-key pop and disco interpolations on singles — so the two records are authored as `modes` (`hard core` and `interpolation single`) rather than averaged into one that is neither. Authoring modes replaces the boom-bap parent's dusty/jazzy/hard set, which is intended: these are the two records this name actually makes. The parameter that is constant across both is the countermelody: \"sung female hook is structural\", not decorative, so `densityRatio` is 0.7 — the highest in this batch — and the string/harp glisses the research names as sweeteners are why `arp` sits alongside the answer lick rather than being absent. `timbreHint` is `bright_bell` for that harp-and-gliss colour. Drums are \"mixed for radio, less dry than the Wu/Mobb end\", authored as a high `quantizeStrength` with moderate velocity variance and a clap layered onto the backbeat. ⚠ `bassline` is authored at the top level with a mid `followRootsProb`, and the modes pull it apart: the single mode moves the bass (\"replayed bass with melodic movement on singles\") and the hardcore mode pins it (\"static sub on hardcore cuts\"). ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high; album production is well documented but the tempo band is catalog-level, and the swing amount is inferred.",
